  "articlePanels": [{"kind":"lede","html":"<p>Fieldwire is a construction task and plan management tool. So the honest reason to look for a <strong>fieldwire alternative uk</strong> teams will use is usually not that Fieldwire is weak. It is that Fieldwire manages drawings and site tasks, and some teams do not need that. They need inspections and compliance captured on site.</p><p>Here is a fair comparison, where Fieldwire is the right choice, and where a no-app capture layer wins.</p>"},{"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>Fieldwire is a capable construction field-management tool: plan viewing and mark-up, task assignment, punch lists and site coordination. Pricing is per-user with a free entry tier and paid plans above it.</li><li>If you need to manage construction plans and tasks against drawings, Fieldwire is the right category and does it well. We say that plainly.</li><li>Quickler is not a smaller, cheaper Fieldwire. It is a different category: the no-app layer that captures inspections and compliance records on site.</li><li>Quickler runs in the worker's existing WhatsApp chat. No app, no login.</li><li>Quickler is £20 per active user a month, dormant users free, only pay for who works, everything unlimited on a paid account.</li><li>Pricing figures for Fieldwire are approximate. Check their current pricing before deciding.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Credit where due","heading":"What Fieldwire does well","html":"<p>An honest comparison starts where the product is strong, and Fieldwire is strong on a construction site.</p><p>It is built around the drawing. You can view and mark up plans, pin tasks and issues to exact locations, assign them to the right person, and track punch lists to completion. For a site team coordinating a build across trades and subcontractors, keeping everything anchored to the plan is genuinely powerful, and general tools do not match it.</p><p>It works offline on site and syncs when signal returns, which matters on a half-built structure. If you run construction projects and need to manage plans, tasks and snagging in one place, Fieldwire is a capable, well-regarded choice. None of that is in doubt.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The real difference","heading":"It is site management, not a capture layer","html":"<p>The difference between Fieldwire and Quickler is not that one is better. It is that they are different categories. Fieldwire manages plans and site tasks. Quickler captures one thing well: inspections and compliance records, on site, without an app.</p><p>Fieldwire is an app, as any plan-and-task tool must be, because it needs the drawing on screen. That is exactly right for its job. For inspection and compliance capture specifically, though, it is still an app to open and a login to keep alive. The check gets done, the app gets filled in later from memory, and the record reflects what someone remembered.</p><p>Quickler does not try to replace plan and task management. If that is what you need, Fieldwire is the right answer and a capture layer will not stand in for it. Quickler solves the narrower problem of getting the on-site compliance record captured reliably in the first place.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The alternatives","heading":"Site tools, paper, and WhatsApp","html":"<p><strong>Fieldwire</strong> is the plan-and-task tool done well: view and mark up drawings, assign tasks, run punch lists. Pick it when you need to manage a construction build against the plan, not just inspections. It is the right category for that job.</p><p><strong>Paper and spreadsheets</strong> stay common in UK teams. Free, familiar, no training. The records are illegible, unsearchable, and impossible to produce when a client or regulator asks. The cost is not the subscription. It is the liability when the folder cannot be found.</p><p><strong>Quickler</strong> removes the app for the capture step. The inspection workflow arrives in the worker's existing WhatsApp chat and they reply to each question as a message. Same behaviour they already do all day, no install, no login. The tradeoff is that quickler is not a plan or task manager and does not try to be.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The no-app option","heading":"Quickler, and where it stops","html":"<p>Quickler runs in WhatsApp. Nothing to install, no login. The inspection or compliance workflow arrives in the worker's existing WhatsApp chat, and they reply to each question as a message. Voice notes are transcribed automatically. The core output is a shareable web report and a permanent record, with optional PDF and CSV export. Pricing is only for active users: [[price:bundles]]. An active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, so you add your whole team and only pay for who actually works. Everything is unlimited on a paid account, so anyone who reads but never files adds nothing to the cost.</p><p>The honest limit: quickler does not manage drawings, tasks or punch lists, and it does not pin issues to a plan. If you need any of that, Fieldwire is the more complete product for a construction build and there is no contest. Quickler is built for teams of roughly 1 to 30 doing structured compliance and inspection capture. It serves care homes, nurseries, schools, estates, cleaning firms and trades, anyone who inspects or keeps records, not construction sites alone. Rooted in UK compliance, it works anywhere WhatsApp does. If you need to manage plans and tasks, Fieldwire is the right tool. If you just need the on-site record captured, that is what quickler does.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Scope, install, price","heading":"Direct comparison","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Scope</th><th>App install?</th><th>Pricing model</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fieldwire</td><td>Construction plan and task management</td><td>Yes, login required</td><td>Per user, free tier plus paid plans</td></tr><tr><td>SimPro</td><td>Full job management and FSM</td><td>Yes, login required</td><td>Quote-based, platform tier</td></tr><tr><td>Joblogic</td><td>Full field-service management</td><td>Yes, login required</td><td>Per user, quote-based</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quickler</strong></td><td><strong>Inspection and compliance capture</strong></td><td><strong>No, WhatsApp only</strong></td><td><strong>£20 per active user/mo, dormant users free</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Pricing is approximate. Check each vendor's current pricing before deciding. The deciding test is simple: do you need to manage plans and tasks on a build, or just capture inspections reliably on site? If the former, Fieldwire is the right category. If the latter, a WhatsApp-native workflow removes the app barrier because your team already uses WhatsApp. See <a href=\"/pages/simpro-alternative-uk.html\">SimPro alternative UK</a> and <a href=\"/pages/joblogic-alternative-uk.html\">Joblogic alternative UK</a>.</p>"},{"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>Is Fieldwire any good?</h3><p>Yes. Fieldwire is a capable construction field-management tool covering plan viewing and mark-up, task assignment, punch lists and site coordination, and it works offline on site. If you need to manage plans and tasks against drawings, it is a well-regarded choice. We would not tell you otherwise.</p><h3>What is the difference between Fieldwire and Quickler?</h3><p>They are different categories. Fieldwire is a plan and task manager built around construction drawings. Quickler is a lightweight capture layer that gets inspections and compliance records captured on site, in WhatsApp, with no app and no login. Quickler does not manage drawings or punch lists and is not trying to replace Fieldwire.</p><h3>Is Quickler cheaper than Fieldwire?</h3><p>They are not really comparable on price because they do different jobs. Fieldwire is priced per user with a free entry tier and paid plans above it. Quickler charges £20 per active user a month, where an active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, and dormant users are free. If all you need is inspection capture, quickler is lighter. If you need construction plan and task management, Fieldwire is doing a different job.</p><h3>Should I switch from Fieldwire to Quickler?</h3><p>Usually not as a straight swap, because they do different things. If you rely on Fieldwire for drawings, tasks and snagging, keep it. Some teams add quickler alongside it purely for on-site inspection and compliance capture, so the record gets caught reliably without another app. Quickler has a free tier of 20 reports a month, up to 10 photos per report, no card and no trial clock. Related: <a href=\"/pages/joblogic-alternative-uk.html\">Joblogic alternative UK</a> and <a href=\"/pages/alternatives.html\">all comparisons</a>.</p>"}],
