  "seoBody": "<h2>Is Fieldwire a good product?</h2><p>Yes. We will say that plainly. Fieldwire is a capable construction field-management tool. Plan viewing and mark-up, task assignment pinned to exact locations, punch lists and site coordination all live together, and it works offline on a half-built site and syncs when signal returns. For a site team coordinating a build across trades, keeping everything anchored to the drawing is genuinely powerful.</p><p>So the honest reason firms go looking for a Fieldwire alternative is rarely that Fieldwire is weak. It is that Fieldwire manages drawings and site tasks, and some teams do not need that. They need inspections and compliance captured reliably on site.</p><h2>The real difference</h2><p>Fieldwire and Quickler are different categories. Fieldwire manages plans and site tasks. Quickler captures one thing well: inspections and compliance records, on site, without an app.</p><p>Fieldwire is an app, as any plan-and-task tool must be, because it needs the drawing on screen. That is exactly right for its job. For inspection capture specifically, though, it is still an app to open and a login to keep alive. The check gets done, the app gets filled in later from memory, and the record reflects what someone remembered rather than what they observed.</p><p>Quickler does not try to replace plan and task management. If that is what you need, Fieldwire is the right answer and a capture layer will not stand in for it.</p><h2>The alternatives</h2><h3>Fieldwire</h3><p>The plan-and-task tool done well: view and mark up drawings, assign tasks, run punch lists. Pick it when you need to manage a construction build against the plan, not just inspections. It is the right category for that job and general tools do not match it.</p><h3>Paper and spreadsheets</h3><p>Still common in UK teams. Free, familiar, no training. The records are illegible, unsearchable, and impossible to produce when a client or regulator asks. The cost is not the subscription. It is the liability when the folder cannot be found.</p><h3>Quickler</h3><p>Quickler runs in WhatsApp. There is nothing to install and no login. The inspection or compliance workflow arrives in the worker's existing WhatsApp chat, and they reply to each question as a message, the same way they reply to anything else. Voice notes are transcribed automatically. The core output is a shareable web report and a permanent record, with optional PDF and CSV export.</p><p>Pricing is only for active users: [[price:bundles]]. An active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, so you add your whole team and only pay for who actually works. Everything is unlimited on a paid account.</p><p>The honest limitation: quickler does not manage drawings, tasks or punch lists, and it does not pin issues to a plan. For a construction build, Fieldwire is the more complete product. Quickler is built for teams of roughly 1 to 30 doing structured compliance and inspection capture, and it serves care homes, nurseries, schools, estates, cleaning firms and trades, anyone who inspects or keeps records, not construction sites alone. Rooted in UK compliance, it works anywhere WhatsApp does.</p><h2>Direct comparison</h2><p><strong>Fieldwire:</strong> construction plan and task management; app install and login required; per user, free tier plus paid plans.</p><p><strong>SimPro:</strong> full job management and FSM; app install and login required; quote-based pricing at the platform tier.</p><p><strong>Joblogic:</strong> full field-service management; app install and login required; per-user, quote-based.</p><p><strong>Quickler:</strong> inspection and compliance capture; £20 per active user/mo, dormant users free, only pay for who works; WhatsApp only, no install; UK-based, direct founder contact.</p><p>Pricing is approximate. Check each vendor's current pricing before deciding.</p><h2>How to choose</h2><p>The deciding test is simple: do you need to manage plans and tasks on a build, or just capture inspections reliably on site?</p><p>If you need to view and mark up drawings, assign tasks and run punch lists, Fieldwire is the right category and a capture layer will not replace it.</p><p>If your problem is narrower, that inspections and compliance checks are not being captured reliably because opening an app on site is one step too many, a WhatsApp-native workflow removes the barrier because your team already uses WhatsApp for everything else.</p><p><strong>One thing to check before deciding:</strong> some teams run both, Fieldwire for the build and quickler for the compliance record. They are not mutually exclusive.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is Fieldwire any good?</h3><p>Yes. Fieldwire is a capable construction field-management tool covering plan viewing and mark-up, task assignment, punch lists and site coordination, and it works offline on site. If you need to manage plans and tasks against drawings, it is a well-regarded choice.</p><h3>What is the difference between Fieldwire and Quickler?</h3><p>They are different categories. Fieldwire is a plan and task manager built around construction drawings. Quickler is a lightweight capture layer that gets inspections and compliance records captured on site, in WhatsApp, with no app and no login. Quickler does not manage drawings or punch lists.</p><h3>Is Quickler cheaper than Fieldwire?</h3><p>They are not really comparable on price because they do different jobs. Fieldwire is priced per user with a free entry tier and paid plans above it. Quickler charges £20 per active user a month, where an active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, and dormant users are free. If all you need is inspection capture, quickler is lighter. If you need construction plan and task management, Fieldwire is doing a different job.</p><h3>Should I switch from Fieldwire to Quickler?</h3><p>Usually not as a straight swap, because they do different things. If you rely on Fieldwire for drawings, tasks and snagging, keep it. Some teams add quickler alongside it purely for on-site inspection and compliance capture. Quickler has a free tier of 20 reports a month, up to 10 photos per report, no card and no trial clock.</p><h2>Try this in Quickler</h2><p>Start free, no card, no trial clock. Copy and paste this into the workflow description when you sign up at <a href=\"https://app.quickler.co/signup\">app.quickler.co/signup</a>:</p><p><code>Fieldwire alternative for inspection capture. Workflow name: [Your inspection or compliance check name]. Description: Describe the on-site check you need captured. Include: what is being inspected, number of questions, whether photos are required, and what the output looks like. Quickler builds it as a WhatsApp workflow. £20 per active user a month, dormant users free, only pay for who works.</code></p><p>Quickler reads your description and builds the WhatsApp question sequence. Your team answers on site. Report produced automatically.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/simpro-alternative-uk.html\">SimPro alternative UK</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/joblogic-alternative-uk.html\">Joblogic alternative UK</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/fergus-alternative-uk.html\">Fergus alternative UK</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/alternatives.html\">All comparisons</a></li></ul>",
